WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:00.800
Vsauce!

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:09.200
Kevin here, with a homemade deck of 52 meme
cards to show you a game that should be perfectly

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.040
fair… but actually allows you to win most
of the time.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:14.560
How?

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:15.680
BECAUSE.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.120
There’s a hidden trick in a simple algorithm
that if you know it, makes you the overwhelming

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.960
favorite even though it appears that both
players have a perfectly equal 50/50 chance

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:27.680
to win.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.920
Well, that’s not very fair.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.280
What is fair?

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.520
We can consider a coin to be “fair” because
it’s binary: it has just two outcomes when

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:44.000
you flip it, heads or tails, and each of those
outcomes are equally probable.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:54.800
Although... it could land on its edge… in
1993, Daniel Murray and Scott Teale posited

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.800
that an American nickel, which has a flat,
smooth outside ridge, could theoretically

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.120
land on its edge about 1 in every 6,000 tosses.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.600
But for the most part, since the first electrum
coins were tossed in the Kingdom of Lydia

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.360
in 7th century BC, they’ve been pretty fair.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.200
As are playing cards, like&nbsp;
this deck of hand-crafted&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:17.920
meme legends.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.120
When you pull a card, you get a red or a black
card.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:25.520
Crying Carson.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.280
It’s perfectly binary, and there’s no
way for a card to like, land on its edge.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.520
It’s either red or it's black.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.320
It's 50% like a snap from Thanos.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.840
Given that, is it possible&nbsp;
to crack the theoretical&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.800
coin-flipping code and take advantage of a

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.600
secret non-transitive property within this
game?

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.100
Yes.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.680
Welcome to the Humble-Nishiyama Randomness
Game.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:59.760
Walter Penney debuted a simple coin-flipping
game in the October 1969 issue of The Journal

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.600
of Recreational Mathematics, and then Steve
Humble and Yutaka Nishiyama made it even simpler

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.360
by using playing cards.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:13.200
The first player chooses a sequence of three
possible outcomes from our deck of cards,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.720
like red, black, red.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.800
And then the second player chooses their own
sequence of three outcomes.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.640
Like black, black, red.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.600
And then we just flip our red and black meme
cards and the winner is the one whose sequence

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:26.880
comes up first.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.240
So in this example, thanks to Guy Fieri, player
one would've won this game because player

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.840
one chose red, black, red.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.040
Here's a little bit of a nitpick.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.760
Because we’re not replacing the red and
black cards after each draw, the probability

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:50.000
won’t be exactly 50/50 on every draw -- because
each time we remove one colored card, the

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.800
odds of the opposite color coming next is
slightly higher -- but it’ll never be far

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.680
from perfectly fair, and as we play it will
continue to balance out.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:06.000
So given that each turn of the card has a
roughly equal chance of being red or black,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.800
and given that the likelihood of each sequence
of three is identical, the probability that

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.920
both players have an equal chance of winning
with their red-and-black sequences has to

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.440
be 50/50, too, right?

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.960
Wrong -- and to demonstrate why I’ve invited
my best friend in the whole wide world.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.560
Where are you best friend?

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.840
Keanu Reeves.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:34.880
Ah, alright.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.320
Keanu, you're a little tall.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:38.820
Hold on.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:42.720
How's this?

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.720
Okay, Keanu will be player 1.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:04:00.647
There are only 8 possible sequences that Keanu
can choose: RRR, RRB, RBR, RBB, BRR, BRB,

00:04:00.647 --> 00:04:01.120
BBR, and BBB.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:07.200
No matter what Keanu chooses, the probability
of that sequence hitting is equal to all the

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.080
other options.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:14.960
For player 1, there really is no bad choice,
one choice is as good as the next.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.400
So let’s say Keanu chooses BRB.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:20.640
Great choice there, Keanu.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.840
Now that I know your sequence, I’m going
to choose BBR.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:32.240
Okay, now we'll just draw some cards and see
which sequence appears first.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.240
Red, Tommy Wiseau.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.960
Some red Flex Seal action.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.320
So far nobody has an advantage.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.200
Black, where are you fingers?

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.800
Uh oh.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.360
Sad, sad Keanu.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:58.640
You should be sad once you realize that now
there's no way that I can lose.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:04.000
Because of having these two black cards in
a row, even if I pull five more black cards

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.840
in a row, eventually I will get a red and
I will win.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:10.900
Ermergerd.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.200
Ah hah.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.320
There it is.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.640
Minecraft Steve had sealed the victory for
me.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.360
Sorry, my most excellent dude.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:24.960
But I win.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.960
Because regardless of what player one chooses,
what matters is the sequence that player 2

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:31.600
picks.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:38.480
As player 2, the method here is very easy
-- I just put the opposite of the middle color

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:45.840
at the front of the line, so when Keanu picked
BRB, I changed his middle R to a B, and then

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.480
put that in the front of my sequence.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.120
So I just dropped the last letter and my sequence
becomes BBR.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.120
I'll show you another example.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.360
If Keanu had chosen red, red, red.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:03.040
Then I would've just changed that middle R
to a B, put that at the beginning of my sequence,

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:07.680
drop the last R, and my sequence would be
BRR.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:15.520
Just that little trick allows me to have an
advantage anywhere from about 2 to 1 up to

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.440
7.5 to 1.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:23.440
Which means in the worst possible scenario
for me, I win 2 out of 3 times.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.240
And in the best, it’s nearly 8 out of 9.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.720
Look, I’ll write out all the choice options
and their odds.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:36.560
As Player 2, when we apply the algorithm we’re
jumping into exactly the right place in a

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.800
cycle of outcomes that Player 1 doesn’t
have any control over.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.760
The best Player 1 can do is choose an option
that’s the least bad.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:48.960
How is this possible?

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:56.640
How can I take something so seemingly fair
to both players, so obviously 50/50, and turn

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.040
it so strongly in my favor?

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.800
The key is in recognizing that this game is
non-transitive.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:08.720
So there ya go.The end.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:10.000
Wait…

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.520
What is transitive?

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:18.800
Think of it this way: you’ve got A, B, and
C. A beats B, and B beats C. Therefore, A

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:25.440
beats C. Because if A beats B and B beats
C then obviously A can beat C. That game sequence

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:26.960
is transitive.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:32.320
So like if you and your Keanu had transitive
food preferences, you'd rather have Pizza

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:36.240
than Tacos, and you’d rather have Tacos
than Dog Food.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.000
You’d also rather have Pizza than Dog Food.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:40.720
Simple.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.280
If you and Keanu somehow preferred Dog Food&nbsp;

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:49.520
to Pizza, then all of a&nbsp;
sudden your food preferences

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:51.760
become non-transitive.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.920
In a non-transitive game, there is no best
choice for the first player because there’s

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:05.280
no super-powered A. Instead, there’s a loop
of winning choices… like rock, paper, scissors.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:11.120
In rock paper scissors, rock -- which we’ll
call A -- loses to paper, which we'll call

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:19.360
B. B is better than A. But A beats scissors,
which is C. So A is better than C.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:26.960
But B loses to C, so C is better than B, and
paper B beats rock A, so B is better than

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:27.440
A.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:35.040
Scissors C loses to rock A and beats paper
B -- and we’ve got a loop of possible outcomes

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:41.920
that goes on forever, with no one choice being
stronger than the other.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:44.400
That’s non-transitive.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.040
Since we’re in the flow chart mood here’s
a flow chart that illustrates the player 2

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:52.560
winning moves in the Humble-Nishiyama Randomness
game.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:09:00.000
So if you follow the arrows you can see that
like RBB beats BBB and like BBR beats BRB.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:00.960
And so forth.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:07.840
With the odds added, you can clearly see how
some sequence scenarios go from bad to worse.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:15.040
In the Humble-Nishiyama Randomness variation
of Penney’s Game, we know what sequence

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:20.960
of card colors player one has chosen first,
so we can jump in the most advantageous part

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:27.200
of the non-transitive loop and make a choice
that gives us a significant advantage.

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:34.080
By recognizing that the game is non-transitive,
we take seemingly-obvious fairness and find

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.640
a paradoxical loophole that nearly guarantees
us success.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:42.080
To everyone who doesn’t&nbsp;
recognize the intransitivity,&nbsp;

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.800
it just kinda looks like we’re extremely

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:45.360
lucky.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:47.840
And why does all of this matter?

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:51.760
Because bacteria play rock paper scissors
to multiply.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:54.480
Benjamin Kirkup and Margaret Riley found that&nbsp;

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:58.320
bacteria compete with one&nbsp;
another in a non-transitive

00:09:58.320 --> 00:09:58.720
way.

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:04.960
They found that in mice intestines, E. coli
bacteria formed a competitive cycle in which

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:11.840
three strains basically played a game of rock
paper scissors to survive and find an equilibrium.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.120
Penney’s Game and its variations illustrate&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:19.440
how even a scenario that seems&nbsp;
perfectly straightforward,

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:25.120
like unmistakably simple, should never be
taken at face value.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:31.600
There’s always room to develop, strategize,
and improve our odds if we put in the effort

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:37.200
and imagination required to understanding
the situation.

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.040
And that truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:44.320
is…breathtaking.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:59.840
And as always -- thanks for watching.

